Solomon was not a prophet; he was a king. He made mistakes—it’s right there in the Bible—as his wives turned his heart toward the worship of idols in his old age.

The core issue here lies between Jesus Christ—who claimed and affirmed that He is the Messiah, the Savior, the Son of God, and God Himself (for God is Father, Son, and Holy Spirit—not three, but one)—to whom the scriptures also point;

And Muhammad, who enters a cave, converses with a demon disguised as an angel, and emerges with a set of laws and a perfect system for persuading warriors to follow him. In an era when warriors desired women, he promised them women; when they wanted wine, he promised them wine; when they sought the spoils of war, he promised them spoils. The men of that time longed for a paradise defined by carnal pleasures—women and wine—and that is precisely the paradise he promised. As for women and children, he offers them nothing; they are merely treated as spoils of war—naturally, since women and children do not constitute an army!

